{"kind":"assert","text":"Read-only foundation preparation at c59b04a014494395865753c957ae37c1959a00ef, tree 02fd8786a979ec68716c4ccbc3fe9572ca6d1153, against landed main 3b468cd04c5cd3925592fed28d9e801323c92702. Tracked under existing request b8c5a3c8, producer update 5d2e1719 and checker preparation 0b7129ca. This is not a filed delivery, reviewguard verdict, implementation closure or selection of the unanswered 29d sequencing contract.\n\nRoot and an independent reader freshly FULL-read both changed bodies and complete native U3: packages/platform/src/destination-objects.ts and packages/platform/test/edit.test.ts. Four unique units, 28,781 UTF8 bytes/419LF; eight independently read units, 57,562 bytes/twelve assigned displays. Character/UTF8 offsets, slice/whole hashes, LF/EOF and every immutable native body/diff passed. Whitespace check exits0. Combined audit SHA256 43a8ac40de25c6b064b0d720823f0c5b3a53823f1ec6fa5cce10cb5b06d6623c. Root FULL-read sealed support report SHA256 5d78c52d8398b75c60b6d2c362f6a6fbf910902999cfd295cdb7913fa275a216. The reader additionally FULL-read current canonical/string-value dependencies; Root directly read the relevant canonicalization and package/export anchors. FULL and PARTIAL credit remain separate.\n\nWithin the stated verified-input, valid-base domain, no blocking tree-construction defect is established. editTree overlays newly made objects before consulting the base closure, so writes sharing a folder preserve earlier writes. Each affected tree retains unrelated entries and uses Git's bytewise row order, treating a directory name as if followed by slash. The final tree is independent of the order of distinct writable paths. Empty input, more than64 files, exact duplicate paths, illegal relative paths and file/folder conflicts refuse the whole returned result. Writing an existing regular file preserves100644/100755; a new file is100644. Touched directories, symlinks and gitlinks, and non-directory intermediary paths, are refused. No delete or mode-change input is introduced.\n\nThe singleton editObjects wrapper calls editTree then the unchanged editCommit. Blob hashing, tree serialization, mode preservation, parent/time/scope/operation commit construction and object insertion order preserve the previous one-file construction structurally. This is static source correspondence, not a newly executed golden-byte result or gate.\n\nThe authored tests meaningfully compare two-file output with Git's independently built index/tree in SHA1 and SHA256, check changed paths/shared folders, reverse submission order and distinguish a singleton result. Duplicate/prefix/empty refusals are asserted. Existing one-file Git object/hash/fsck/parent/message/path witnesses remain. Facts and readings are manufactured test fixtures; no host, push, founding, required-check execution or deployment follows. No tests were executed by checker. Producer names tested foundation d3f94e89, but supplies no focused raw log or final gate in this update. Native d3f94e89-to-c59 delta is only the twenty-two already landed documentation/sample paths; that connects code identity, not missing runtime evidence.\n\nA suspected encoded-name alias was withdrawn for signed inputs. editTree checks JS-string uniqueness before encoding names, and the bare utf8 helper is a TextEncoder wrapper. Current canonicalize/strict JSON parser and text-value guards reject lone UTF16 surrogates, so admitted signed text cannot retain the suspect strings. Unchecked direct callers still owe the declared precondition. No surrogate vector, simulation or generated test was run. The anonymous primary check confirms the encoder API uses USVString; the actual admitted-input guarantee comes from current project guards. [Encoding Standard](https://encoding.spec.whatwg.org/#interface-textencoder)\n\nThree concrete follow-ups for the actual delivery:\n- Returned objects retain intermediate roots/ancestor trees from sequential writes and may vary with input order even though final tree is canonical. They are not promised to be a minimal or canonical pack. Sequential reconstruction repeats directory scans, sorts and serialization across the files; the64-path ceiling alone does not bound base-tree size, aggregate bytes, retained objects or latency. Keep actual capacity/transport bounds explicit.\n- The current package exports only its root and testing entry points. Its root exports editCommit/editObjects/editPath, but not new editTree/TreeFile; tests import the implementation directly. Shared cross-package manifest/destination use needs the supported public seam when its contract is selected. This does not prevent the present internal singleton wrapper.\n- Existing tests do not exercise64/65, executable/gitlink policy, reverse-prefix refusal, incomplete closure/object format or staging/payload limits. These are affected-boundary follow-ups, not a request for duplicated whole suites or mutation sweeps. Authenticate the supplied base/closure and match every signed path/content/size/digest in the owning layers; this helper does not establish those checks.\n\nArchitecture: pure tree construction separates content agreement from commit identity, review/check authority and publication. Existing singleton behavior shares the same implementation. It does not settle declared-digest versus native-version selection, collecting several propose-file entries under current manifest guards, or computing and binding the check subject before reserve. Those three questions remain 29d's owner decision; no guessed alias or interface is adopted here.\nSecurity: relative path/type/mode protections remain and staging has no outside write. Strict scalar-string guards resolve the candidate alias. Base integrity, exact signed intent, authority/current-rule/check binding, custody and capacity remain separate responsibilities; no malformed-input, private-source, provider or complete security assurance is inferred.\nSimplification: one shared builder and a small commit wrapper avoid two independent serializers. The explicit overlay is readable. Preserve exact bytes and meaningful evidence when later addressing intermediate-object/work costs.\n\nThe complete b8 delivery still owes current adopted field/sequence correspondence, both commands sharing the actual manifest tree, a required check on a two-file change, observed deployed published/refused outcomes, final source-correct gate, filed artifacts and independent guarded review, requester judgment and witnessed landing. Binary/delete/rename pack support stays in the named later R5 design; full functional scope is not reduced by this preparation. Native-room/history/custody/retention/capacity and prior broader product duties remain open.\n\nMethod qualifications: one Root wrapper displayed two assigned source slices plus a small rg anchor search. An auxiliary index display was tool-truncated and gets no FULL credit; a fresh bounded index24-35 read supplies the export anchor. Assigned BODY/U3 ledgers are complete independently of those auxiliary views. No project/module/script execution/import/eval/compiler/tests/control/gate/build/install, generated inputs/Git-vector simulation, browser/provider/model/audio/private configuration/process/kernel probe, raw gitseq envelope decoding, source edit or Git mutation.","body":{"head":"c59b04a014494395865753c957ae37c1959a00ef","scope":"Independent static preparation only; no reviewguard verdict or implementation closure"}}